schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Impact Certificates & Hypercerts
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Introduces hypercerts as tokens claiming "this group did this work, here,
    during this time" and retroactive funding as paying after impact is
    demonstrated rather than upfront, describing the creator/funder/evaluator
    roles, the split-and-merge property, and links to quadratic funding,
    RetroPGF, and MRV as connected ReFi tools.
  audience: >-
    community organizers and funders new to impact-certificate / retroactive-
    funding mechanisms
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      pay-after-proof-reduces-funder-risk-vs-creators-still-need-working-capital-before-impact-exists
  related_concepts:
    - hypercert-erc-1155-impact-claim-token
  related_resources: []
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/impact-certificates-hypercerts.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    assessed-but-excluded: "Hypercerts Protocol" was not re-created as a new
    resource object — it already exists in data/kb/resource.yaml from a prior
    ingestion slice. "Retroactive Public Goods Funding (RetroPGF / Retro
    Funding)" was not re-created as a new concept-lineage object for the same
    reason, and the existing claim-evidence "Retroactive funding pays for
    demonstrated impact, not promises" already covers the general retroactive-
    funding thesis — this ingestion instead captures the ERC-1155 hypercert
    token structure and the specific (uncited) ProjectTogether example. See
    the accompanying signal for a likely factual error in the article
    ("Built on the AT Protocol").
